CSE-4114 Compiler Design Project
Formal Grammar for অঙ্কুর (Ankur)

Notation: ::= means "is defined as", | is alternation, { x } means zero-or-more
repetitions of x, [ x ] means x is optional, terminals are quoted.

Implementation status: every rule below is implemented by the lexer, parser,
and semantic analyzer, and every statement form is handled by all three code
generators -- Java (ankur.codegen.JavaCodeGenerator), Python
(ankur.codegen.PythonCodeGenerator) and WebAssembly
(ankur.codegen.WasmCodeGenerator). The "Compiling to ..." sections below give
the three source-to-target mappings. Nothing in this grammar is aspirational;
docs/Ankur-Final-Report.md's Future Roadmap covers what's deliberately not
in the language yet (booleans as a declarable type, functions, string
operators).

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Program
------------------------------------------------------------------------

<program>          ::= "শুরু" <statement-list> "শেষ"

<statement-list>    ::= { <statement> }

<statement>         ::= <var-decl>
                       | <assignment>
                       | <if-stmt>
                       | <while-stmt>
                       | <print-stmt>

<block>             ::= "শুরু" <statement-list> "শেষ"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Declarations and Statements
------------------------------------------------------------------------

<var-decl>          ::= <type> <identifier> [ "=" <expression> ] ";"

<type>               ::= "পূর্ণ"        (* int *)
                        | "দশমিক"      (* float *)
                        | "বাক্য"       (* string *)

<assignment>         ::= <identifier> "=" <expression> ";"

<if-stmt>            ::= "যদি" "(" <expression> ")" <block>
                          [ "নাহলে" <block> ]

<while-stmt>         ::= "যতক্ষণ" "(" <expression> ")" <block>

<print-stmt>         ::= "দেখাও" "(" <expression> ")" ";"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Expressions (precedence climbing, lowest to highest precedence)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

<expression>         ::= <logical-or>

<logical-or>         ::= <logical-and> { "||" <logical-and> }

<logical-and>        ::= <equality> { "&&" <equality> }

<equality>           ::= <relational> { ( "==" | "!=" ) <relational> }

<relational>         ::= <additive> { ( "<" | ">" | "<=" | ">=" ) <additive> }

<additive>           ::= <multiplicative> { ( "+" | "-" ) <multiplicative> }

<multiplicative>     ::= <unary> { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) <unary> }

<unary>              ::= ( "!" | "-" | "+" ) <unary>
                        | <primary>

<primary>            ::= <int-literal>
                        | <float-literal>
                        | <string-literal>
                        | <identifier>
                        | "(" <expression> ")"

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Lexical Grammar
------------------------------------------------------------------------

<identifier>          ::= <id-start> { <id-part> }
<id-start>            ::= bangla-letter | ascii-letter
<id-part>             ::= <id-start> | <digit> | "_"

<int-literal>         ::= <digit> { <digit> }
<float-literal>       ::= <digit> { <digit> } "." <digit> { <digit> }
<string-literal>      ::= '"' { <string-char> } '"'
<string-char>         ::= any character except '"', backslash or newline
                          | "\\" ( '"' | "\\" | "n" | "t" )
<digit>               ::= bangla-digit | ascii-digit

bangla-letter          ::= any character in Unicode U+0980..U+09FF that is
                           not a bangla-digit
bangla-digit            ::= "০" | "১" | "২" | "৩" | "৪" | "৫" | "৬" | "৭" | "৮" | "৯"
                            (Unicode U+09E6..U+09EF)
ascii-letter            ::= "a".."z" | "A".."Z" | "_"
ascii-digit             ::= "0".."9"

comment                 ::= "//" { any character except newline } newline

Whitespace (space, tab, carriage return, newline) and comments are not
significant and may appear between any two tokens. Numeral scripts may be
mixed freely: ৫ and 5 both denote the integer five.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Data Types (course requirement 2.1 asks for two; Ankur has three)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

পূর্ণ    (Purno)     -- integer type
দশমিক   (Doshomik)  -- floating point type
বাক্য    (Bakko)     -- string type

A বাক্য can be declared, assigned and printed, but has no operators at all:
concatenation would mean building a new string at run time, and the
WebAssembly target has no allocator to build one with (its strings are
constant bytes in linear memory). See the Future Roadmap in
docs/Ankur-Final-Report.md.

A fourth, non-declarable type, BOOLEAN, exists only as the result of
relational (==, !=, <, >, <=, >=) and logical (&&, ||, !) operators. It
cannot be stored in a variable in this version of the language -- see the
"Future Roadmap" note in docs/Ankur-Final-Report.md. যদি/যতক্ষণ conditions
must be of type BOOLEAN; a bare number is a semantic error.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Type Checking Rules (implemented in ankur.semantic.SemanticAnalyzer)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. A variable must be declared before use, and not already declared in
   the current scope.
2. Arithmetic operators (+ - * / %) require both operands to be numeric
   (পূর্ণ or দশমিক). Result is দশমিক if either operand is দশমিক, else পূর্ণ.
3. Relational operators require both operands numeric; result is BOOLEAN.
4. Logical operators (&& || !) require BOOLEAN operand(s); result is
   BOOLEAN.
5. Assignment (including a var-decl initializer) allows পূর্ণ -> দশমিক
   widening, but rejects দশমিক -> পূর্ণ narrowing as a type error. A বাক্য
   may only be assigned a বাক্য: there is no conversion in either direction.
   দেখাও accepts any of the three types.
6. Each block (if/else/while body) introduces a new scope; an inner
   declaration may shadow an outer one of the same name without error.
7. A পূর্ণ literal must fit in a 32-bit signed int (matching the Java `int`
   it compiles to); out of range is a semantic error, not a codegen crash.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Compiling to Java (ankur.codegen.JavaCodeGenerator)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

পূর্ণ -> int, দশমিক -> double. Operators map 1:1 to Java's own symbols, so
no translation table is needed there. Every declared name keeps the
author's own Bangla identifier in the generated Java verbatim -- EXCEPT a
genuine shadow: Ankur allows a nested block to shadow an outer variable
(rule 6 above), but Java rejects redeclaring a name already in an
enclosing scope, so only that inner, shadowing declaration is renamed
("<name>_<n>"), the one place reusing the source name directly would make
the Ankur program fail to compile.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Compiling to Python (ankur.codegen.PythonCodeGenerator)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The other target course requirement 2.1 allows. পূর্ণ -> int, দশমিক -> float,
বাক্য -> str, and যদি/নাহলে/যতক্ষণ map to if/else/while. Two things do not
translate literally:

  /               Python's / always produces a float, so পূর্ণ / পূর্ণ is
                  emitted as // (integer division) to match Ankur's rule 2

  variables       Python has no block scope at all -- a name assigned
                  inside an if is the same variable as one outside it --
                  so a declaration that actually shadows an outer one gets
                  renamed, exactly as in the Java target; an ordinary,
                  non-shadowing declaration keeps the author's own name

&& and || become and / or, ! becomes not, and an empty block becomes pass,
since Python takes its block structure from indentation.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Compiling to WebAssembly (ankur.codegen.WasmCodeGenerator)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The optional target of course requirement 2.2. পূর্ণ -> i32, দশমিক -> f64.
Output is both the text format (.wat) and a binary module (.wasm) encoded
directly by ankur.codegen.wasm.WasmBinaryWriter, so no external assembler
is needed.

WebAssembly is a typed stack machine rather than a structured high-level
language, so the mapping is less direct than the Java one:

  expression      operands are pushed first, the operator instruction
                  consumes them (post-order), e.g. ক + খ becomes
                  local.get, local.get, i32.add

  int -> float    emitted explicitly as f64.convert_i32_s. WebAssembly has
                  no implicit conversion, so every place rule 2 or rule 5
                  above allows a পূর্ণ where a দশমিক is expected needs one

  variables       every declaration gets its own function-level local
                  index (locals must all be declared at the top of a
                  function), which is also what makes shadowing work

  যদি / নাহলে      if / else / end

  যতক্ষণ           block { loop { <cond> i32.eqz br_if 1 <body> br 0 } }
                  -- there is no while instruction; the block supplies the
                  exit target and the loop supplies the back edge

  দেখাও            call to a host function imported as "অঙ্কুর"."দেখাও_পূর্ণ"
                  or "দেখাও_দশমিক" -- a module has no I/O of its own

  BOOLEAN         an i32 of 0 or 1, which is how WebAssembly itself models
                  conditions

  && and ||       a structured if that yields an i32, not i32.and/i32.or,
                  because Ankur's operators short-circuit and the plain
                  instructions would evaluate both sides

  দশমিক %          a - trunc(a / b) * b, via scratch locals: WebAssembly
                  has i32.rem_s but no f64 remainder instruction. পূর্ণ %
                  maps straight to i32.rem_s

  বাক্য            there is no string type. Every literal's UTF-8 bytes go
                  into the module's data segment, and a বাক্য value is a
                  (pointer, length) pair of i32s -- so a বাক্য variable
                  occupies two locals, and দেখাও_বাক্য takes two arguments.
                  The memory is exported so the host can read the bytes
                  back out and decode them

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Intermediate Representation (ankur.tac.TacGenerator)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Between semantic analysis and the code generators, the validated AST is
also lowered to three-address code: every expression is flattened into one
operation per instruction over named operands (t1, t2, ...), and যদি and
যতক্ষণ become explicit labels and jumps.

  যদি (c) A নাহলে B     ifFalse c goto L1 / A / goto L2 / L1: / B / L2:
  যতক্ষণ (c) B          L1: / ifFalse c goto L2 / B / goto L1 / L2:

The three code generators walk the AST rather than this list, because all
three targets have structured control flow of their own and would only have
to rebuild it from the jumps. TAC is the representation an optimiser or a
real machine backend would start from, and it is written to
generated/<Name>.tac on every run.
